Phase 6 (Local AI), first slice: the evidence-handle contract - execution
order §4.10's actual fix for why both previously-measured models failed
(docs/evidence/27-ai-eval-corrected-sweep.log: best result 2/6 graded
cases, with Phi-4-mini fabricating an event ID in two others - "dominated
by malformed/fabricated event-ID references, not reasoning quality").

## 1. Environment check before writing any code

Before building runtime/model-manager infrastructure, checked whether this
machine can safely run the actual multi-gigabyte model download and
benchmark ADR 0006 requires: `df -h` showed 5.3GB free on this machine's
only drive. A Qwen3-4B-Instruct Q4_K_M GGUF alone is several GB; the
low-resource and comparator candidates ADR 0006 names would not fit
alongside it, and even attempting one risks filling a shared primary work
machine's disk to zero - a hard-to-reverse, high-blast-radius action this
project's own safety rules single out for care, not something to attempt
speculatively. Checked the second physical test machine instead (already
authorized for "freely within its safety rules" per the standing
decisions this mission runs under): `C:` 85.9GB free, `D:` 204.3GB, `F:`
172.7GB - ample room for the full three-candidate evaluation. The actual
download/benchmark run moves there; this machine builds and tests the
code that run depends on, with zero download risk.

## 2. What was actually wrong with the prior design

`ai/eval/harness.ModelOutput.EvidenceEventIDs` and the system prompt both
worked directly with real event IDs (ULIDs) - the harness graded a cited
ID as fabricated only if it did not appear in `case.EventIDs`, which
requires the model to correctly reproduce a ~26-character ULID verbatim to
pass at all. `ai/eval/run/main.go`'s JSON schema was also a build-time
constant meant for `llama-server -jf <file>` at server startup - which
fixes one schema for the whole server session and cannot vary per case.

## 3. What was built

`ai/eval/harness/evidence.go` (new): `HandleMap` (`BuildHandles`,
`RedactEvent`, `ResolveHandles`). Each event gets a short handle ("E1",
"E2", ...) in input order; `RedactEvent` returns a copy with `event_id`
replaced by `handle` and `schema_v`/`ts_mono` (analytically meaningless)
stripped - every other field (kind, subject, attrs, evidence, severity,
confidence, ts_wall, source, observer_id, count) passes through
unchanged, so the fix removes only the field that caused fabrication, not
information the model used to reason.

`harness.ModelOutput.EvidenceEventIDs` -> `EvidenceHandles`; `Grade` now
takes a `HandleMap` instead of a bare `known-IDs` set, resolves the
model's cited handles back to real IDs via `ResolveHandles`, and reports
any handle outside the offered set as a violation by name - structurally
the same hard-fail severity as a fabricated ULID always was, but now
checked against a small closed set instead of a large ID space.

`ai/eval/run/main.go`: `jsonSchemaFor(handles []string)` builds the
per-case schema with `evidence_handles` as a closed `enum` of exactly the
handles offered for that case - sent per-request via
`response_format.json_schema` (llama-server's OpenAI-compatible
structured-output field) instead of the old server-wide `-jf` flag,
because only a per-request field can vary the enum case to case as
execution order §4.10 actually specifies ("a dynamic JSON-schema/grammar
built per-request from the actual evidence set"). The events shown to the
model are now `hm.RedactEvent(e)` for every event, not the raw event -
the real ULID is never present anywhere in the prompt at all, so there is
nothing shaped like a real ID left to fabricate a plausible variant of.
The system prompt was rewritten to talk about handles instead of event
IDs throughout.

## 4. Full regression

    $ go build ./...                               clean
    $ go vet ./...                                  clean
    $ CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./...                   all packages pass (ai/eval/harness, ai/eval/run, and every pre-existing package unaffected)
    $ CGO_ENABLED=0 go test ./ai/eval/harness/... -v   12 tests, including all 33 real generated cases individually

## 5. Proven, not just passing once

Two things proven by deliberate breakage, not merely asserted:
- `TestAHandleOutsideTheOfferedSetIsAlwaysAViolation`: the actual
  `hm.ResolveHandles` call in `Grade` was replaced with a version that
  always reports zero unknown handles (`var unknownHandles []string`,
  discarding the real result) - the test failed with exactly "a handle
  never offered for this case must never pass", confirming the test
  depends on that check firing, not on some unrelated code path. Restored
  immediately after (`git diff --stat` shows zero remaining diff from the
  pre-break state, confirming a clean restore).
- `TestRedactEventNeverLeavesTheRealEventIDReachable`: marshals the
  redacted event to JSON and asserts the real ULID string is not a
  substring anywhere in the output - a positive proof the anti-
  fabrication fix holds at the source (the model is never shown the ID at
  all), not just that the field was renamed.

`TestHarnessRunsAgainstEveryGeneratedCase` re-ran against all 33 real
cases from `ai/eval/cases/` (not a synthetic subset) with the new
handle-based grading and passed every one - confirming the refactor
integrates correctly across the entire existing corpus, not only
hand-picked fixtures.

## 6. What remains before a real benchmark run is possible

Guardrails not yet built: the deterministic confidence-ceiling
computation (currently only graded as an upper bound in the harness, not
computed and enforced by a real backend), forced `insufficient_evidence`
on a required-collector-down/in-window gap, and DNS/IP redaction before
prompt construction (the existing `-deidentified` case variant proves the
concept works for lab addresses, but the real evidence-bundle privacy
toggle this is supposed to reuse is not wired in yet). The Tauri sidecar
(pinned llama.cpp binary, loopback/random-port/session-token/RAM/thread/
context/timeout limits) and the model manager (signed manifest, resumable
download, hash+signature verification, quarantine) do not exist yet
either - both needed before any UI, and both pure-code work with no
download risk, so next.

The actual model download and CPU benchmark run itself is deferred to the
second test machine (§1) - not attempted here.
